{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba ../agent-source/docs/README.md | sed -n '1,74p'; nl -ba ../agent-source/raft.go | sed -n '845,870p;963,987p;1032,1094p'; python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('../submission.schema.json'))['\"'$defs'\"']; print(json.dumps(s['Grounding'],indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Raft Developer Documentation\n     2\t\n     3\tThis documentation provides a high level introduction to the `hashicorp/raft`\n     4\timplementation. The intended audience is anyone interested in understanding\n     5\tor contributing to the code.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Contents\n     8\t\n     9\t1. [Terminology](#terminology)\n    10\t2. [Operations](#operations)\n    11\t   1. [Apply](./apply.md)\n    12\t3. [Threads](#threads)\n    13\t\n    14\t\n    15\t## Terminology\n    16\t\n    17\tThis documentation uses the following terms as defined.\n    18\t\n    19\t* **Cluster** - the set of peers in the raft configuration\n    20\t* **Peer** - a node that participates in the consensus protocol using `hashicorp/raft`. A\n    21\t  peer may be in one of the following states: **follower**, **candidate**, or **leader**.\n    22\t* **Log** - the full set of log entries.\n    23\t* **Log Entry** - an entry in the log. Each entry has an index that is used to order it\n    24\t  relative to other log entries.\n    25\t  * **Committed** -  A log entry is considered committed if it is safe for that entry to be\n    26\t    applied to state machines. A log entry is committed once the leader that created the\n    27\t    entry has replicated it on a majority of the peers. A peer has successfully\n    28\t    replicated the entry once it is persisted.\n    29\t  * **Applied** - log entry applied to the state machine (FSM)\n    30\t* **Term** - raft divides time into terms of arbitrary length. Terms are numbered with\n    31\t  consecutive integers. Each term begins with an election, in which one or more candidates\n    32\t  attempt to become leader. If a candidate wins the election, then it serves as leader for\n    33\t  the rest of the term. If the election ends with a split vote, the term will end with no\n    34\t  leader.\n    35\t* **FSM** - finite state machine, stores the cluster state\n    36\t* **Client** - the application that uses the `hashicorp/raft` library\n    37\t\n    38\t## Operations\n    39\t\n    40\t### Leader Write\n    41\t\n    42\tMost write operations must be performed on the leader.\n    43\t\n    44\t* RequestConfigChange - update the raft peer list configuration\n    45\t* Apply - apply a log entry to the log on a majority of peers, and the FSM. See [raft apply](apply.md) for more details.\n    46\t* Barrier - a special Apply that does not modify the FSM, used to wait for previous logs to be applied\n    47\t* LeadershipTransfer - stop accepting client requests, and tell a different peer to start a leadership election\n    48\t* Restore (Snapshot) - overwrite the cluster state with the contents of the snapshot (excluding cluster configuration)\n    49\t* VerifyLeader - send a heartbeat to all voters to confirm the peer is still the leader\n    50\t\n    51\t### Follower Write\n    52\t\n    53\t* BootstrapCluster - store the cluster configuration in the local log store\n    54\t\n    55\t\n    56\t### Read\n    57\t\n    58\tRead operations can be performed on a peer in any state.\n    59\t\n    60\t* AppliedIndex - get the index of the last log entry applied to the FSM\n    61\t* GetConfiguration - return the latest cluster configuration\n    62\t* LastContact - get the last time this peer made contact with the leader\n    63\t* LastIndex - get the index of the latest stored log entry\n    64\t* Leader - get the address of the peer that is currently the leader\n    65\t* Snapshot - snapshot the current state of the FSM into a file\n    66\t* State - return the state of the peer\n    67\t* Stats - return some stats about the peer and the cluster\n    68\t\n    69\t## Threads\n    70\t\n    71\tRaft uses the following threads to handle operations. The name of the thread is in bold,\n    72\tand a short description of the operation handled by the thread follows. The main thread is\n    73\tresponsible for handling many operations.\n    74\t\n   845\t\t\t\t\t}\n   846\t\t\t\t}\n   847\t\n   848\t\t\tcase v := <-r.verifyCh:\n   849\t\t\t\tr.mainThreadSaturation.working()\n   850\t\t\t\tif v.quorumSize == 0 {\n   851\t\t\t\t\t// Just dispatched, start the verification\n   852\t\t\t\t\tr.verifyLeader(v)\n   853\t\t\t\t} else if v.votes < v.quorumSize {\n   854\t\t\t\t\t// Early return, means there must be a new leader\n   855\t\t\t\t\tr.logger.Warn(\"new leader elected, stepping down\")\n   856\t\t\t\t\tr.setState(Follower)\n   857\t\t\t\t\tdelete(r.leaderState.notify, v)\n   858\t\t\t\t\tfor _, repl := range r.leaderState.replState {\n   859\t\t\t\t\t\trepl.cleanNotify(v)\n   860\t\t\t\t\t}\n   861\t\t\t\t\tv.respond(ErrNotLeader)\n   862\t\n   863\t\t\t\t} else {\n   864\t\t\t\t\t// Quorum of members agree, we are still leader\n   865\t\t\t\t\tdelete(r.leaderState.notify, v)\n   866\t\t\t\t\tfor _, repl := range r.leaderState.replState {\n   867\t\t\t\t\t\trepl.cleanNotify(v)\n   868\t\t\t\t\t}\n   869\t\t\t\t\tv.respond(nil)\n   870\t\t\t\t}\n   963\t// verifyLeader must be called from the main thread for safety.\n   964\t// Causes the followers to attempt an immediate heartbeat.\n   965\tfunc (r *Raft) verifyLeader(v *verifyFuture) {\n   966\t\t// Current leader always votes for self\n   967\t\tv.votes = 1\n   968\t\n   969\t\t// Set the quorum size, hot-path for single node\n   970\t\tv.quorumSize = r.quorumSize()\n   971\t\tif v.quorumSize == 1 {\n   972\t\t\tv.respond(nil)\n   973\t\t\treturn\n   974\t\t}\n   975\t\n   976\t\t// Track this request\n   977\t\tv.notifyCh = r.verifyCh\n   978\t\tr.leaderState.notify[v] = struct{}{}\n   979\t\n   980\t\t// Trigger immediate heartbeats\n   981\t\tfor _, repl := range r.leaderState.replState {\n   982\t\t\trepl.notifyLock.Lock()\n   983\t\t\trepl.notify[v] = struct{}{}\n   984\t\t\trepl.notifyLock.Unlock()\n   985\t\t\tasyncNotifyCh(repl.notifyCh)\n   986\t\t}\n   987\t}\n  1032\t// checkLeaderLease is used to check if we can contact a quorum of nodes\n  1033\t// within the last leader lease interval. If not, we need to step down,\n  1034\t// as we may have lost connectivity. Returns the maximum duration without\n  1035\t// contact. This must only be called from the main thread.\n  1036\tfunc (r *Raft) checkLeaderLease() time.Duration {\n  1037\t\t// Track contacted nodes, we can always contact ourself\n  1038\t\tcontacted := 0\n  1039\t\n  1040\t\t// Store lease timeout for this one check invocation as we need to refer to it\n  1041\t\t// in the loop and would be confusing if it ever becomes reloadable and\n  1042\t\t// changes between iterations below.\n  1043\t\tleaseTimeout := r.config().LeaderLeaseTimeout\n  1044\t\n  1045\t\t// Check each follower\n  1046\t\tvar maxDiff time.Duration\n  1047\t\tnow := time.Now()\n  1048\t\tfor _, server := range r.configurations.latest.Servers {\n  1049\t\t\tif server.Suffrage == Voter {\n  1050\t\t\t\tif server.ID == r.localID {\n  1051\t\t\t\t\tcontacted++\n  1052\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n  1053\t\t\t\t}\n  1054\t\t\t\tf := r.leaderState.replState[server.ID]\n  1055\t\t\t\tdiff := now.Sub(f.LastContact())\n  1056\t\t\t\tif diff <= leaseTimeout {\n  1057\t\t\t\t\tcontacted++\n  1058\t\t\t\t\tif diff > maxDiff {\n  1059\t\t\t\t\t\tmaxDiff = diff\n  1060\t\t\t\t\t}\n  1061\t\t\t\t} else {\n  1062\t\t\t\t\t// Log at least once at high value, then debug. Otherwise it gets very verbose.\n  1063\t\t\t\t\tif diff <= 3*leaseTimeout {\n  1064\t\t\t\t\t\tr.logger.Warn(\"failed to contact\", \"server-id\", server.ID, \"time\", diff)\n  1065\t\t\t\t\t} else {\n  1066\t\t\t\t\t\tr.logger.Debug(\"failed to contact\", \"server-id\", server.ID, \"time\", diff)\n  1067\t\t\t\t\t}\n  1068\t\t\t\t}\n  1069\t\t\t\tmetrics.AddSample([]string{\"raft\", \"leader\", \"lastContact\"}, float32(diff/time.Millisecond))\n  1070\t\t\t}\n  1071\t\t}\n  1072\t\n  1073\t\t// Verify we can contact a quorum\n  1074\t\tquorum := r.quorumSize()\n  1075\t\tif contacted < quorum {\n  1076\t\t\tr.logger.Warn(\"failed to contact quorum of nodes, stepping down\")\n  1077\t\t\tr.setState(Follower)\n  1078\t\t\tmetrics.IncrCounter([]string{\"raft\", \"transition\", \"leader_lease_timeout\"}, 1)\n  1079\t\t}\n  1080\t\treturn maxDiff\n  1081\t}\n  1082\t\n  1083\t// quorumSize is used to return the quorum size. This must only be called on\n  1084\t// the main thread.\n  1085\t// TODO: revisit usage\n  1086\tfunc (r *Raft) quorumSize() int {\n  1087\t\tvoters := 0\n  1088\t\tfor _, server := range r.configurations.latest.Servers {\n  1089\t\t\tif server.Suffrage == Voter {\n  1090\t\t\t\tvoters++\n  1091\t\t\t}\n  1092\t\t}\n  1093\t\treturn voters/2 + 1\n  1094\t}\n{\n  \"additionalProperties\": false,\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"source_ids\": {\n      \"description\": \"Exact IDs of acquired implementation source materials; not Behavior or Binding IDs\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      },\n      \"title\": \"Source Ids\",\n      \"type\": \"array\"\n    },\n    \"expectation_ids\": {\n      \"description\": \"Exact IDs of acquired materials supporting the expectation; not invented expectation labels. Explain normative applicability in derivation.\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      },\n      \"title\": \"Expectation Ids\",\n      \"type\": \"array\"\n    },\n    \"binding_ids\": {\n      \"default\": [],\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      },\n      \"title\": \"Binding Ids\",\n      \"type\": \"array\"\n    },\n    \"derivation\": {\n      \"default\": \"\",\n      \"title\": \"Derivation\",\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"applicability\": {\n      \"default\": \"\",\n      \"title\": \"Applicability\",\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"unresolved\": {\n      \"default\": [],\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      },\n      \"title\": \"Unresolved\",\n      \"type\": \"array\"\n    },\n    \"conflicts\": {\n      \"default\": [],\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      },\n      \"title\": \"Conflicts\",\n      \"type\": \"array\"\n    },\n    \"alternatives\": {\n      \"default\": [],\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      },\n      \"title\": \"Alternatives\",\n      \"type\": \"array\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"title\": \"Grounding\",\n  \"type\": \"object\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"The developer documentation describes `VerifyLeader` as confirming leadership through voters. The code sets a voter-majority threshold but accepts Boolean notifications from every registered replication worker, including nonvoters. I’ll define a narrow check for whether nonvoter support can satisfy that threshold, preserving lease expiry and negative replies as protections. A result would not by itself establish a stale read or competing leader."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > build_candidate.py <<'PY'\nimport json\nsources=[dict(id='s-verify-documentation',file='docs/README.md',start_line=38,end_line=49,kind='document_statement'),dict(id='s-verify-tests',file='raft_test.go',start_line=1866,end_line=1972,kind='test_expectation')]\nss=['s-verify-api','s-verify-documentation','s-membership-contract','s-verify-tests','s-leader-loop','s-repl-ownership','s-verify-vote','s-replication','s-heartbeat','s-transfer','s-match-notify']\nq=dict(audit_spec_version=1,disposition='ready_for_check',preferred_check='controlled_schedule',question='In a stable configuration containing three voters and a nonvoter, can a verification future consume the nonvoter worker notification as the missing quorum contribution and complete successfully while no remote voter notification has contributed to that verification?',importance='Verification is a public authority check advertised for avoiding stale FSM reads. Counting a participant that cannot constrain a competing election could weaken that check. The proposed local obligation concerns verification participant eligibility, not proof of a competing election, a stale read, log divergence or overall consensus failure.',source_ids=ss,participants=['Local leader voter','Two remote voters','Configured nonvoter'],objects=['verifyFuture','followerReplication.notify','latest Configuration'],contexts=['One leader-state generation','Stable membership with three voters and one nonvoter','One independently identified verification future'],event_paths=['Public VerifyLeader -> leaderLoop -> verifyLeader -> per-worker registration -> heartbeat/replication reply -> notifyAll -> verifyFuture.vote -> leaderLoop -> future.Error'],activity_classes=['A2'],behavior_ids=['b-heartbeat','b-notify-replication','b-verify','b-leader-life'],fact_ids=['f-verification-notification'],supporting_behavior_ids={'b-config':'Stable membership determines worker suffrage and voter-majority threshold.','b-campaign':'A real elected-leader prefix can replace assumed leader state.','b-lease':'Voter-contact lease can terminate the leader and must remain active or be explicitly bounded in an isolated consumer comparison.','b-append':'Actual recipient can produce heartbeat success without voting eligibility.','b-replicate':'Alternative successful append path drains the same pending notifications and must be controlled or counted.'},obligation_relation_kind='consumption',counterevidence=['Lease counts only voters and eventually demotes an isolated leader.','Any delivered negative verification notification can cause failure before threshold completion.','Worker notification map draining and voteLock prevent repeated final notification; duplicate counting is not the current proposition.','Single-voter clusters legitimately complete from self support.','A successfully verified peer is not proven to have lost leadership merely because it cannot reach other voters at an instant.'],unknowns=['No same-execution admitted verification, supporter identities or endpoint have been measured.','An executed real election/configuration prefix is needed for a reachable-cluster conclusion; direct leader-state construction would establish only a conditional consumer result.','Transport controls must account for already in-flight ordinary append and heartbeat replies; a partition call alone does not prove that no voter notification contributes.','Broader stale-read consequence requires a distinct later-term election and observed application endpoint.'],priority=3,trigger_rationale='Accepted map establishes an all-peer registration loop with voter-only threshold and a notification object that drops suffrage. Newly acquired developer documentation explicitly identifies voters as VerifyLeader recipients; healthy/partial-connect tests support majority availability rather than an all-voters response requirement.')\nclaim='o-verify-voter-eligibility'\nbind=[]\nfor id,mat,sym,a,b,desc in [('bind-verify-register','s-leader-loop','Raft.verifyLeader',965,986,'Threshold and replication-worker registration'),('bind-verify-tally','s-verify-vote','verifyFuture.vote',274,293,'Boolean notification aggregation and terminal re-enqueue'),('bind-verify-result','s-leader-loop','Raft.leaderLoop',848,869,'Public future result from tally'),('bind-verify-producer','s-replication','followerReplication.notifyAll',100,110,'Worker-associated notification before peer eligibility is discarded')]:\n bind.append(dict(id=id,material_id=mat,symbol=sym,start_line=a,end_line=b,description=desc,pending=[],associations=[dict(claim_id=claim,source_ids=[mat],rationale=desc+' for the same verification future.')]))\no=dict(id=claim,kind='obligation',concern='implementation_semantics',description='For a verification in an unchanged configuration with three voters including the local leader, successful quorum completion must not be obtained from local self support plus only nonvoter worker notifications. At least one distinct remote configured voter must contribute positive support to that verification before successful completion.',source_ids=ss,scope=dict(description='Participant-eligibility check of one verification future in one leader generation under fixed three-voter plus one-nonvoter membership. Compare nonvoter-only remote positive support against a permitted remote-voter control. This is a necessary-condition check, not an eventual-success or all-voters requirement.',assumptions=['Operation identity and worker suffrage are independently recorded and fixed during each comparison.','No remote voter contribution is inferred from connectivity alone: pending registrations, reply delivery and alternate successful append paths must be accounted for.','The executed artifact must declare whether authority/configuration prefix is real or supplied. Supplied state yields only a conditional local result.','Network loss/delay is non-Byzantine; replies used as positive support must come from actual implementation or be identified as a conditional substitute.'],excluded=['A later leader election or stale FSM read unless separately observed.','Configuration transitions, self removal, shutdown and disaster recovery during the comparison.','General liveness failure from an unfinished verification or tool timeout.']),pending=['Construct and execute a correlated check with nonvoter and voter control cases.'],grounding=dict(source_ids=['s-leader-loop','s-repl-ownership','s-verify-vote','s-replication','s-heartbeat','s-transfer'],expectation_ids=['s-verify-documentation','s-verify-api','s-membership-contract','s-verify-tests'],binding_ids=[b['id'] for b in bind],derivation='Developer documentation describes VerifyLeader heartbeats to voters to confirm authority, while the API promises an authority check usable to prevent stale reads. Membership declarations distinguish voting participants from nonvoters. The implementation threshold and partial-connect test clarify majority support, not unanimity. Together these support the necessary eligibility condition: a nonvoter notification cannot substitute for the remote voter needed in a three-voter verification quorum. Code asymmetry motivates the comparison but is not itself the normative source. No eventual success is required when eligible support is absent.',applicability='Applies to ordinary VerifyLeader with stable membership including nonvoters; this is an implementation authority-check contract. No requirement that a disconnected node instantly changes its local role is asserted.',unresolved=['Documentation does not define a linearization point or complete read protocol; these remain outside the local eligibility claim.','Measurement of producer identity, registration and terminal public result remains required.'],conflicts=[],alternatives=['Lease may terminate the old leader later, but does not qualify an earlier nonvoter contribution.','Negative support can reject verification; schedule must preserve or finitely delay this path and report its handling.','Current authority might in fact persist despite lost communication; the obligation constrains verification support rather than asserting a competing leader.']))\ns=dict(action='obligation',rationale='Select notification consumption at the authority-verification boundary as the first discriminator on the accepted map. The new documentation supplies applicable expectation beyond code asymmetry. Register a narrow necessary-condition obligation before constructing its direct check.',sources=sources,question=q,obligation=o,bindings=bind,feedback=dict(ref_ids=['s-verify-documentation','s-verify-tests','s-leader-loop','s-repl-ownership','s-verify-vote'],answered='Source review aligns the lead with voter eligibility in verification, rather than treating loss of connectivity as proof of lost authority or stale reads. Majority support is sufficient in the existing partial-connect test, so the obligation does not require every voter response.',remaining=q['unknowns'],understanding='updated',rationale='The accepted notification lifecycle and context already express the relevant producer/consumer relation; no map revision is necessary to add this scoped contract. Real-producer execution with correlated supporter identities is the next discriminator. Heartbeat term and pipeline error leads remain separate frontier options.'))\njson.dump(s,open('verify-obligation.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\npython3 build_candidate.py\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,subprocess\nr=json.load(open('../research.json')); p=subprocess.run(r['validation']['command']+['verify-obligation.json'],capture_output=True,text=True);print(p.stdout); print(p.stderr)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
